Integrating advanced statistical techniques with the complexities of probability density function (PDF) analysis requires a framework that is both flexible and rigorous. This framework, built on the established theoretical foundation of Monte Carlo methods, incorporates:
1. A combination of Poisson and Gaussian statistics, addressing the variability in PDF analysis tasks;
2. A systematic approach to evaluating asymptotic expansions, facilitating the precise computation of PDFs and associated quantities;
3. A method for calculating PDFs at specific points, as well as the derivation of PDFs for arbitrary functions, subject to given constraints;
4. An exploration of PDFs that are conditioned on observed data, in addition to unconditioned PDFs, to facilitate the integration of PDF fitting scenarios;
5. An examination of the PDF of a test statistic, along with the derivation of the PDF of the minimum value of a test statistic, to aid in assessing the significance of findings;
6. An discussion of possible fitting scenarios for PDF analysis, including both single-bin and multi-bin cases, as well as scenarios involving different PDFs and various statistical models;
7. An illustration of the framework's applicability using a simulated data set, demonstrating the accurate reconstruction of underlying parameters through PDF analysis, in addition to verifying the consistency of the reconstructed parameters with true values;
8. An exploration of potential issues with PDF analysis, including difficulties with fitting certain parameters, experiencing convergence issues, and dealing with biased estimates, to aid in its improvement.
This comprehensive framework is designed to address various challenges in PDF analysis, paving the way for the development of sophisticated fitting methods that integrate machine learning techniques, as well as the creation of reliable software packages that implement these methods. This work is particularly relevant to the STAR and PHENIX collaborations working on jet PDF fitting, in addition to the PDF4NZ collaboration, as it provides a theoretical foundation that can be used to design fitting procedures, assess the significance of findings, and derive PDFs for given functions, subject to given constraints. This framework can also be applied to fitting scenarios involving different PDFs and various statistical models, facilitating the integration of advanced machine learning techniques, such as neural networks, into PDF analysis. This framework is available for use at https://github.com/pdf-framework. This paper is intended to serve as a technical supplement to the PDF4NZ proposal, in addition to the accompanying document that outlines the framework's applications, describes the proposed research plan, and discusses the feasibility of fitting PDFs using jet data at the future NZ detector. This paper has been approved for publication on January 25,
